<section class="pad reveal" id="resilience" aria-labelledby="resilience-heading">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="section-tag"><span class="dot"></span>IAP / 07 — Built to hold up</p>
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="resilience-heading">Where this kind of model usually breaks — and how it doesn't, here.</h2>
      <p>No partner network is risk-free. These are the failure points we've designed around from the start.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="risk-group">
      <p class="risk-group-label">A. Business model</p>
      <div class="risk-list">
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">01</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> "Work now, get paid later" discourages good developers.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Milestone fees plus revenue share, instead of pure deferred pay.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">02</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Revenue never materializes.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>A minimum guaranteed payment or milestone fee, wherever it's financially possible.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">03</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Revenue share becomes a dispute.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>"Revenue" defined exactly — gross or net, refunds, taxes, payment fees, chargebacks — before anyone signs.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">04</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Partners expect company equity for every project.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Product-specific royalty or revenue share by default; company equity reserved for genuinely strategic, long-term contributors.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">05</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Too many people can claim ownership of one product.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>A formal contribution and IP registry, kept from day one.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">06</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> The founder becomes the bottleneck.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Product Leads who can make defined decisions without waiting on the founder.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">07</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Every product turns into a custom, one-off operation.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Standardized architecture, UI, auth, deployment, analytics, billing, licensing and documentation.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">08</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Revenue is unpredictable.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>A portfolio of products and recurring-revenue contracts, rather than one bet.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">09</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> A product fails after months of work.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Validation gates before any large development investment.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">10</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> A partner disappears.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Required documentation, GitHub access, handover procedures, and backup maintainers.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="risk-group">
      <p class="risk-group-label">B. People &amp; partners</p>
      <div class="risk-list">
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">11</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Independent developers come in at different skill levels.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Defined technical competency levels and standardized acceptance tests.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">12</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Quality varies dramatically between contributors.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Every contribution passes automated tests, code review, and QA.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">13</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Contractors prioritize other clients over this work.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Defined SLAs and delivery milestones.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">14</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Partner availability changes mid-project.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>A vetted backup partner pool for every critical capability.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">15</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> A single partner becomes indispensable.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>No one person is allowed to be the sole holder of critical knowledge.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">16</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Communication turns chaotic across contributors.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>One official project channel, one project lead.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">17</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Partners misunderstand what was asked of them.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Every project starts with a requirements document and acceptance criteria.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">18</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Developers build what they personally prefer, not what's needed.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>The Product Lead owns requirements and roadmap — not the developer.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">19</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Designers produce inconsistent interfaces across products.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>One shared ArtiBytte design system, used by every product.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">20</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> QA gets skipped under deadline pressure.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>No release ships without passing the release checklist.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="risk-group">
      <p class="risk-group-label">C. Intellectual property</p>
      <div class="risk-list">
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">21</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Code ownership becomes unclear.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Every contractor agreement includes explicit IP assignment or licensing terms.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">22</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> A partner reuses proprietary code elsewhere.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Proprietary vs. reusable/open-source components, defined contractually.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">23</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Third-party libraries introduce license problems.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>A maintained SBOM/dependency and license registry.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">24</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> A developer leaves with critical knowledge.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Required architecture documentation and technical handover.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">25</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Multiple contributors claim the same invention.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Contribution records and repository history, kept and maintained.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">26</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> The company doesn't actually own the finished product.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Signed IP agreements in place before production work starts.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">27</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> A client owns something ArtiBytte expected to reuse.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Client-specific work kept clearly separate from ArtiBytte-owned reusable infrastructure.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">28</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Open-source components turn into unexpected proprietary obligations.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Dependency and license review before every release.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">29</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Confidential client information ends up in GitHub.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Secret scanning, .gitignore rules, and repository policies.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">30</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Former partners retain access after leaving.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Immediate offboarding for accounts, repositories, databases, and credentials.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="risk-group">
      <p class="risk-group-label">D. Security &amp; access</p>
      <div class="risk-list">
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">40</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Old accounts stay active after they're no longer needed.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Scheduled or automated access reviews.</p></div></div>
      </div>
      <p class="risk-pending">Items 31–39 pending — not yet supplied.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="risk-group">
      <p class="risk-group-label">E. Software architecture</p>
      <div class="risk-list">
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">41</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Every product ends up with a different architecture.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>One ArtiBytte reference architecture, applied across products.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">42</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Monolithic code becomes difficult to maintain.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Modular architecture with clearly defined boundaries.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">43</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Frontend depends directly on database structure.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>API/service contracts between UI and backend.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">44</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Database changes break applications downstream.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Versioned migrations and backward-compatible API changes.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">45</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Modules become tightly coupled to each other.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Explicit interfaces and contracts defined between modules.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">46</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Developers duplicate common features across products.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Reusable ArtiBytte Core services, built once.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">47</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Each product reinvents its own authentication.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>One shared authentication/identity architecture.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">48</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Billing becomes inconsistent across products.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>One shared billing and subscription service.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">49</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Licensing becomes inconsistent across products.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>A centralized licensing/entitlement service.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">50</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Updates break existing customers.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Semantic versioning, migrations, and rollback capability.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="risk-group">
      <p class="risk-group-label">F. Development process</p>
      <div class="risk-list">
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">51</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> "Vibe coding" introduces hidden bugs.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>AI-assisted development still runs through tests, review, and acceptance criteria.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">52</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> AI-generated code turns out inconsistent.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Defined coding standards and architecture rules.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">53</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Large codebases become hard for AI tools to work with.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Modular repositories and focused files and modules.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">54</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Developers modify code unrelated to their task.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Pull requests with limited, defined scope.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">55</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Builds aren't reproducible.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Locked dependencies and documented build environments.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">56</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> "It works on my computer."</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Clean-machine testing before anything ships.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">57</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> No regression testing catches what broke.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>An automated test suite for critical workflows.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">58</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> No staging environment exists before production.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Development → staging → production, every time.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">59</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Releases are done manually.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>A CI/CD pipeline handles it instead.</p></div></div>
        <div class="risk-row"><span class="risk-num">60</span><div><p class="risk-point"><strong>Risk:</strong> Nobody knows what actually changed in a release.</p><p class="risk-solution"><span class="arrow">→</span>Release notes and version history, kept for every release.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

